# libgtk-4-dev and libvte-2.91-gtk4-dev are here for satellite-term alone; the
# interpreter compiles and links without either, which is the point of the
# split. g++ 12 is the floor because library.hpp's lock-free publish protocol
# is built on std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<const Directory>>, and libstdc++ did
# not implement that specialisation before 12.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               g++ (>= 12),
               libgtk-4-dev,
               libvte-2.91-gtk4-dev,
               pkg-config
Standards-Version: 4.7.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: satellite
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
# ${shlibs:Depends} resolves to libc6, libgcc-s1 and libstdc++6 and to nothing
# else: `ldd satl` lists six objects, five of them the C and C++ runtimes
# and the sixth the loader. Nothing is hardcoded here precisely so that the day
# a GUI library creeps into the interpreter's link line, it shows up in this
# package's Depends and the regression is visible.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Suggests: satellite-term
Description: interpreter for the satellite programming language
 satellite is a small language generated by a single naming rule: a dotted path
 rooted at "satellite" names something the language owns, and a bare identifier
 names something the user owns. Everything else follows from it, including the
 fact that "satellite" is the only reserved word in the language -- "variable",
 "library", "time" and "list" are special only in the second position of a
 satellite-rooted path, so a program is free to call one of its own variables
 "time".
 .
 Functions are capsules and classes are spacesuits. There is one numeric type,
 an exact arbitrary-precision decimal, so the language has no float, no
 rounding mode and no precision question to answer. The implementation is a
 tree-walking interpreter over slot-indexed call frames, with no bytecode: the
 bootstrap plan is to compile satellite to C, and bytecode is the one target
 that would leave a virtual machine behind that could never be deleted.
 .
 This package contains "satl", the interpreter, which links no GUI
 libraries at all. It runs a program headless with "satl --run prog.satl"
 and offers a REPL on any terminal. Install satellite-term as well for the
 GTK 4 terminal window.

Package: satellite-term
Architecture: any
# The interpreter is not merely a sensible companion, it is a runtime
# requirement: satellite-term resolves its own path through /proc/self/exe and
# spawns the sibling "satl" next to it, so an unversioned or absent
# dependency would leave a window that opens and immediately fails to spawn.
Depends: satellite (= ${binary:Version}),
         libgtk-4-1,
         libvte-2.91-gtk4-0,
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
# A Recommends rather than a Depends because window.cpp asks pango for the
# ordered family "IBM Plex Mono,monospace" and an absent Plex falls through to
# the system monospace, so the terminal works without the package and merely
# looks like something else.
Recommends: fonts-ibm-plex
Description: GTK 4 terminal window for the satellite interpreter
 satellite-term is a GTK 4 application owning one VTE terminal, into whose
 pseudo-terminal it spawns the satellite interpreter. Running the interpreter
 behind a real PTY is what supplies echo, backspace, line editing, Ctrl-C,
 Ctrl-D and resize handling: they come from the kernel's tty line discipline
 rather than from several hundred lines of terminal emulation that would
 otherwise have to be written and maintained.
 .
 It is a separate binary from the interpreter, and separately packaged, because
 linking GTK 4 and VTE into the interpreter made the dynamic linker load 119
 shared objects before main() on every invocation. That cost 25.9 ms for a
 hello world whose actual interpretation takes 0.3 ms, against 2.5 ms for the
 same interpreter unlinked and a 2.2 ms bare-process floor. A headless machine
 installs the satellite package on its own and pulls in no GUI libraries
 whatsoever.
